 ### Artifact schemas (`schemas/`)
 
-One JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) per structured artifact at the harness root: `changed-files`, `test-results`, `verification-result`, `retry-guidance`, `story`, `execution-history`. The first five are the single source of truth for the shapes the harness routes on; each is injected into the prompt that asks an agent to produce the artifact *and* read by the coordinator to check what the agent produced, so the two can never drift.
+One JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) per structured artifact at the harness root, named by `schemas/manifest.json`: `changed-files`, `test-results`, `verification-result`, `retry-guidance`, `story`, `execution-history`. The first five are the single source of truth for the shapes the harness routes on; each is injected into the prompt that asks an agent to produce the artifact *and* read by the coordinator to check what the agent produced, so the two can never drift.
 
 `execution-history.schema.json` is the exception that clarifies the rule. Its artifact is coordinator-written rather than stage-written, so it appears in no stage's `schemas` map and no stage is asked to satisfy it, and the coordinator does not validate its own write at run time — a self-check against a shape the same code just produced buys nothing. It gets a schema anyway, because a schema is how this harness defines a shape it routes on *or hands to an agent*, and because `schema_context` makes any file in `schemas/` an injectable placeholder for the consumers that come later. The schema is the definition; the story's tests are what check conformance.
 
-The schemas directory is an *inventory* two tests assert set equality over (`tests/test_schema_validator.py` and `tests/test_story_004_validation.py`). Adding a schema file necessarily fails both, by design — the inventory exists so a new shape cannot appear unnoticed. Expect to update both in the same story, and keep them asserting exact equality rather than relaxing them to a subset.
+`schemas/manifest.json` is the one *declared* inventory: a JSON object with a single `schemas` key holding the sorted list of shipped schema names as kebab-case stems without the `.schema.json` suffix, matching how `load_schema` already names them. It is read through `schema_validator.shipped_schemas()`, and the two inventory tests (`tests/test_schema_validator.py` and `tests/test_story_004_validation.py`) assert exact set equality between the manifest and the directory — both directions bite: a schema file with no manifest entry fails, and a manifest entry with no schema file fails. The inventory exists so a new shape cannot appear unnoticed, so **keep it exact equality; never relax it to a subset or a containment check.** Adding a schema means editing `schemas/` and its manifest, and nothing under `tests/`.
 
-Schemas ship with the harness code (like the orchestration modules), not with per-repository `.harness/` config, so `schema_validator` resolves them relative to its own module rather than a caller-supplied root. `load_schema` accepts an optional override for callers that need one; `context_assembler` still globs the `harness_root` it is passed.
+That placement is the point, not a detail. Before story-013 the inventory was two hand-maintained literals *inside* the test files, which collided head-on with the standing story requirement that the implementer touch no file under `tests/`. story-011 is where the collision landed: it added `execution-history.schema.json`, its `changed-files.json` named three paths under `tests/`, and the verifier raised it as a blocking issue the retry could only record as a deviation. Moving the declaration beside the schemas it declares makes adding a schema entirely implementer territory and lets the `tests/`-independence sentence hold verbatim for every future story. The tests stopped being where the fact is *stored* and became where it is *checked*, which is what a test should be — and the duplicate copy dissolved as a side effect, since both files now read the one manifest.
+
+The manifest is written and read by the same stage, which is weaker than a list a different stage maintains. That is accepted deliberately: it is a flat list of filename stems with a one-line diff, and every name it carries still has to pass the draft-2020-12, unsupported-keyword and no-`additionalProperties` checks parametrized over it, so a bogus entry fails loudly rather than silently widening the inventory. `shipped_schemas` raises on a missing or malformed manifest rather than returning an empty or partial tuple, because an empty inventory would make those parametrized checks silently vacuous.
+
+The inventory globs `*.schema.json` so the manifest is not counted as a schema. The coverage the older `glob("*")` gave for free is preserved by a companion assertion in the same tests: `schemas/` contains nothing except `*.schema.json` files plus `manifest.json`, so a stray file still cannot appear unnoticed. `context_assembler.schema_context` already globs `*.schema.json`, so `manifest.json` is no injectable placeholder and no `{{manifest_schema}}` appears in any rendered prompt. Nothing in orchestration routes on the manifest; `shipped_schemas` has no caller inside `orchestration/`.
+
+Schemas ship with the harness code (like the orchestration modules), not with per-repository `.harness/` config, so `schema_validator` resolves them relative to its own module rather than a caller-supplied root. `schemas_dir`, `load_schema`, and `shipped_schemas` each accept the same optional `harness_root` override for callers that need one — `shipped_schemas` resolves the manifest *through* `schemas_dir`, so the override behaves identically across all three, and a test can stand up a throwaway harness root with its own schemas and manifest. `context_assembler` still globs the `harness_root` it is passed.
 
 `story.schema.json` describes the full story shape — nested story fields, typed arrays, `technical_plan` as a known optional property — and the whole of it is consumed: it both *drives* the parse in `story_parser` and validates the parsed result. It is the one place the ambiguity YAML cannot resolve is resolved: whether `- some criterion: with a colon` is a string or a mapping is decided by `items.type` at the schema node being read, not by the syntax of the line.
 
